Participants complete the questionnaires either at home using their own devices or provided equipment, or at designated study locations. This method provides a comparison baseline against which to evaluate newer digital data collection approaches, representing current standard practice for gathering patient-reported health informat","armGroupLabels":["Control Standard Data Collection Method"],"otherNames":["Traditional Electronic Survey Method","Static Standardized Digital Surveys"]}]},"outcomesModule":{"primaryOutcomes":[{"measure":"Acceptability","description":"Acceptability will be measured using the Acceptability, Feasibility and Usability Survey (AFUS) - Acceptability Subscale, a researcher-developed instrument. This subscale includes items from Part 1 (Core Assessment Experience), Part 2 (culture), Part 3 (Future Use \& Preference), Part 4 (Privacy \& Trust), and Part 5 (Medical Disclosure Comfort). Items are rated on a 7-point Likert scale (1 = Strongly Disagree to 7 = Strongly Agree). Subscale scores will be calculated as the mean of all acceptability items. Higher scores indicate greater acceptability. Subscale breakdown will be accessible in supplemental figures of publications.","timeFrame":"Assessed immediately after completion of data collection procedures (typically within 1-2 weeks of enrollment)"},{"measure":"Usability","description":"Usability will be measured using the Acceptability, Feasibility and Usability Survey (AFUS) - Usability Subscale (Part 6), a researcher-developed instrument adapted from established usability frameworks. The subscale consists of 10 items rated on a 7-point Likert scale (1 = Strongly Disagree to 7 = Strongly Agree). Negatively-worded items are reverse-scored. The total score is calculated as the mean of all items. Higher scores indicate better usability.","timeFrame":"Assessed immediately after completion of data collection procedures (typically within 1-2 weeks of enrollment)"},{"measure":"Technical Feasibility","description":"Technical feasibility will be measured by the proportion of data collection sessions completed without significant technical failures. A significant technical failure is defined as any system error, software malfunction, connectivity interruption, hardware issue, or other technical problem that results in early termination of the session. Technical success rate will be calculated as the number of sessions without significant technical failures divided by total sessions attempted, expressed as a percentage. Higher percentages indicate greater technical feasibility.","timeFrame":"Assessed throughout study participation (typically within 1-2 weeks of enrollment)"},{"measure":"Implementation Feasibility","description":"Implementation feasibility will be measured using the Acceptability, Feasibility and Usability Survey (AFUS) - Future Use \& Preference Subscale (Part 3). Items assess participant willingness to use the data collection method again, likelihood of recommending it to others, and trust in the method for ongoing health evaluation. Items are rated on a 7-point Likert scale (1 = Strongly Disagree to 7 = Strongly Agree). The subscale score is calculated as the mean of all items. Higher scores indicate greater implementation feasibility.","timeFrame":"Assessed immediately after completion of data collection procedures (typically within 1-2 weeks of enrollment)"},{"measure":"Data Collection Feasibility","description":"Feasibility of comprehensive data collection will be measured by the proportion of biopsychosocial domains for which data was successfully collected across all sessions. Domain coverage will be calculated as the number of domains with complete data divided by total possible domains, expressed as a percentage. Domains include biological (pain characteristics, sleep, physical function, nutrition), psychological (mood, cognition, coping, trauma, pain catastrophizing), and social (relationships, support, work, identity). Higher percentages indicate more comprehensive data collection.","timeFrame":"Assessed at completion of all data collection procedures (typically within 1-2 weeks of enrollment)"}],"secondaryOutcomes":[{"measure":"Participant Experience & Satisfaction","description":"Patient experience will be measured using the Acceptability, Feasibility and Usability Survey (AFUS) - Overall Experience Subscale (Part 7). Items are rated on a 7-point Likert scale (1 = Strongly Disagree to 7 = Strongly Agree). The subscale score is calculated as the mean of all items. Higher scores indicate more positive patient experience.","timeFrame":"Assessed immediately after completion of data collection procedures (typically within 1-2 weeks of enrollment)"},{"measure":"Patient Engagement - Session Duration","description":"Patient engagement will be measured by session duration, defined as the total time in minutes participants actively spend on data collection tasks, recorded via system logs. Longer durations indicate greater time engagement with the data collection process.","timeFrame":"Measured during each data collection session (typically within 1-2 weeks of enrollment)"},{"measure":"Patient Engagement - Response Elaboration","description":"Patient engagement will be measured by mean response length, defined as the average number of words per participant response across the conversation transcript. Higher word counts indicate greater verbal engagement with the data collection process. (experimental group only)","timeFrame":"Measured during each data collection session (typically within 1-2 weeks of enrollment)"},{"measure":"Perceived Therapeutic Alliance","description":"Perceived therapeutic alliance with the data collection interface will be measured using the Acceptability, Feasibility and Usability Survey (AFUS) - Therapeutic Alliance Subscale (Module A). Items are rated on a 7-point Likert scale (1 = Strongly Disagree to 7 = Strongly Agree). The subscale score is calculated as the mean of all items. Higher scores indicate stronger perceived therapeutic alliance.","timeFrame":"Assessed immediately after completion of conversational data collection (typically within 1-2 weeks of enrollment)"},{"measure":"Immersion and Comfort","description":"Immersive experience will be measured using the Acceptability, Feasibility and Usability Survey (AFUS) - Immersive Experience Subscale (Module B). Items are rated on a 7-point Likert scale (1 = Strongly Disagree to 7 = Strongly Agree). The subscale score is calculated as the mean of all items. Higher scores indicate better immersive experience.","timeFrame":"Assessed immediately after completion of immersive data collection (typically within 1-2 weeks of enrollment)"}],"otherOutcomes":[{"measure":"Correlation Between Physiological Measures and Engagement","description":"The association between physiological arousal and participant engagement will be measured using correlation coefficients between Empatica-derived physiological measures (electrodermal activity, pulse rate) and engagement metrics (time spent answering questions, response word count). Correlation coefficients range from -1.0 to 1.0, with values further from zero indicating stronger associations.","timeFrame":"Assessed during in-person data collection sessions (typically within 1-2 weeks of enrollment)"},{"measure":"Correlation Between Physiological Measures and Self-Reported Affect","description":"The association between physiological measures and self-reported emotional states will be measured using correlation coefficients between Empatica-derived data (electrodermal activity, pulse rate, skin temperature) and participant-reported affective states from the transcripts and standardized questionnaires. Correlation coefficients range from -1.0 to 1.0, with values further from zero indicating stronger concordance between physiological and self-reported measures.","timeFrame":"Assessed during in-person study sessions (typically within 1-2 weeks of enrollment)"},{"measure":"Emotive & Linguistic Analysis","description":"Four linguistic metrics will be derived from transcripts: (1) Response Elaboration-mean words per response, with higher counts indicating greater engagement; (2) Linguistic Complexity-Flesch-Kincaid Grade Level score (0-18+) representing text sophistication; (3) Vocabulary Diversity-Type-Token Ratio (unique words/total words, 0-1.0); and (4) Self-Referential Language-percentage of first-person singular pronouns (I, me, my, mine) calculated via LIWC-22, with higher percentages associated with depression and pain catastrophizing.","timeFrame":"Assessed at completion of the data collection (typically within 1-2 weeks of enrollment)"},{"measure":"Sentiment Valence of Participant Responses","description":"Overall sentiment will be measured using automated sentiment analysis of transcripts. Sentiment scores will be calculated using validated natural language processing methods, yielding a score ranging from -1.0 (entirely negative) to +1.0 (entirely positive), with 0 representing neutral sentiment. Higher scores indicate more positive sentiment.","timeFrame":"Assessed at completion of the data collection (typically within 1-2 weeks of enrollment)"},{"measure":"Thematic Content Categories","description":"Thematic content of participant responses will be analyzed using qualitative thematic analysis of conversation transcripts. Two independent coders will identify recurring themes using an inductive approach, with coding disagreements resolved through consensus discussion. Inter-rater reliability will be assessed using Cohen's kappa for theme identification and reported. Results will be reported as the number of participants whose transcripts contain each identified theme (expressed as n and percentage). More prevalent themes indicate more commonly shared experiences across participants.","timeFrame":"Assessed at completion of conversational data collection (typically within 1-2 weeks of enrollment)"},{"measure":"Topic Responsiveness","description":"Participant responsiveness to questions will be measured by the percentage of questions that receive on-topic responses. Two independent coders will rate each participant response as on-topic or off-topic relative to the preceding prompt. Inter-rater reliability will be assessed using Cohen's kappa and reported. Discrepancies will be resolved through consensus discussion. Higher percentages indicate greater topic responsiveness and engagement with the structured conversation.","timeFrame":"Assessed at completion of conversational data collection (typically within 1-2 weeks of enrollment)"},{"measure":"Disclosure Depth","description":"Depth of participant self-disclosure will be measured using a researcher-developed coding scheme applied to transcripts. Two independent coders will rate each response on a 4-point scale (0 = no disclosure, 1 = factual/surface disclosure, 2 = moderate personal disclosure, 3 = deep emotional disclosure). Mean disclosure depth will be calculated across all responses. Inter-rater reliability will be assessed using intraclass correlation coefficient (ICC) and reported. Discrepancies will be resolved through consensus discussion. Higher scores indicate deeper self-disclosure.","timeFrame":"Assessed at completion of conversational data collection (typically within 1-2 weeks of enrollment)"},{"measure":"Convergent Validity - Depression","description":"Convergent validity will be assessed by comparing depression-related outputs from the data collection methods to the Patient Health Questionnaire-8 (PHQ-8). Validity will be measured using Pearson or Spearman correlation coefficients. Correlation coefficients range from -1.0 to 1.0, with values closer to 1.0 indicating stronger convergent validity.","timeFrame":"Assessed at completion of data collection procedures (typically within 1-2 weeks of enrollment)"}]},"eligibilityModule":{"eligibilityCriteria":"Inclusion Criteria:

